  'pretoria-gauteng-heatwave-warning-2026-11-11',
  'Heatwave to hit Pretoria and Gauteng this week, SAWS warns',
  'Community', '2026-11-11', 'Eyewitness News', 'https://www.ewn.co.za/2026/11/11/sa-weather-service-warns-of-scorching-el-nino-heatwave-and-veld-fire-risks',
  'SAWS warned of a three-day heatwave across Gauteng from 11 to 13 November, with Pretoria forecast to reach 34 degrees Celsius and veld fires possible in parts of three provinces.',
  'The South African Weather Service has warned residents across Gauteng, including Pretoria, to prepare for scorching conditions from Wednesday until Friday this week, driven by a high-pressure system parked over the region. Eyewitness News reported that Pretoria was due to hit 34 degrees Celsius on Thursday under an extreme ultraviolet rating of 13, with similarly hot weather forecast for the Northern Cape and Free State and a danger of veld fires breaking out in parts of those provinces.

A heatwave is officially declared when a town or city records maximum temperatures at least five degrees above its usual average for the hottest month, on three or more consecutive days, according to the weather service. Rekord reported that this week''s spell also stretches into North West, the rest of the Free State, the Northern Cape, Limpopo''s Bushveld region, the Mpumalanga Highveld and the far north-west of KwaZulu-Natal. A weather service forecaster linked the heat to an established El Nino system over southern Africa and said more such spells could follow this summer.

Health officials warn that staying out in such heat for long stretches can bring on dehydration, exhaustion and, in severe cases, heatstroke. Residents are being told to keep hydrated, stick to shaded or cool spots, and steer clear of strenuous activity in the hottest part of the day. Schools have been asked to cancel outdoor sport and physical education while the warning is in effect, and parents are being told to keep children''s outdoor play away from the 10am-to-4pm window. People working outdoors should take frequent breaks, cover their heads and wear light, loose clothing, while anyone caring for young children, elderly relatives or pets is urged to watch them closely for signs of heat strain.',
  NULL, NULL, NULL,
  '["https://www.ewn.co.za/2026/11/11/sa-weather-service-warns-of-scorching-el-nino-heatwave-and-veld-fire-risks", "https://www.citizen.co.za/rekord/news-headlines/2026/11/11/heatwave-warning-pretoria-braces-for-three-days-of-intense-heat/"]',
  'agent'
);
